A recursive call can move through the original `next` links first. Once the call returns, the current node is placed after its old next node by swapping its two pointers.

The empty list is handled before recursion. A single node also works because both links are already `null`.

```java
/*
class ListNode {
    int data;
    ListNode prev, next;
    ListNode(int val) {
        this.data = val;
        this.prev = null;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    public ListNode reverseDLL(ListNode head) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode newHead = reverseDLL(head.next);

        ListNode oldNext = head.next;
        head.next = head.prev;
        head.prev = oldNext;

        return newHead;
    }
}
```

The original forward link is saved in `oldNext` because the links are changed after the recursive call. The input nodes are reused and their links are modified.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack.

The list can be reversed by changing both links of every node. For each node, `next` and `prev` are swapped. After the swap, the old previous node is the next node to process.

The returned head is the last node visited. The list is changed in place, so no new nodes are created.

```cpp
/*
class ListNode {
public:
    int data;
    ListNode* prev;
    ListNode* next;

    ListNode(int val) : data(val), prev(nullptr), next(nullptr) {}
};
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseDLL(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode* current = head;
        ListNode* newHead = nullptr;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            ListNode* oldNext = current->next;
            current->next = current->prev;
            current->prev = oldNext;

            newHead = current;
            current = oldNext;
        }

        return newHead;
    }
};
```

For `1 <-> 2 <-> 3`, node `1` gets `next = null`, node `2` points to `1`, and node `3` becomes the new head. The final order is `3 <-> 2 <-> 1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The nodes are collected in forward order, then connected from the last array position back to the first. This keeps the node objects and only changes their `prev` and `next` fields.

The method does not create replacement nodes. It only needs one array, and the returned node has its `prev` set to `null`.

```javascript
/*
class ListNode {
    constructor(data) {
        this.data = data;
        this.prev = null;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
*/

class Solution {
    reverseDLL(head) {
        if (head === null) {
            return null;
        }

        const nodes = [];
        let current = head;

        while (current !== null) {
            nodes.push(current);
            current = current.next;
        }

        for (let i = nodes.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            const node = nodes[i];
            const nextIndex = i - 1;
            const prevIndex = i + 1;

            node.next = nextIndex >= 0 ? nodes[nextIndex] : null;
            node.prev = prevIndex < nodes.length ? nodes[prevIndex] : null;
        }

        return nodes[nodes.length - 1];
    }
}
```

A quick dry run with `4 <-> 7`: the array is `[4, 7]`. For node `7`, `next` becomes `4` and `prev` becomes `null`. For node `4`, `next` becomes `null` and `prev` becomes `7`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

